## ⚙️ Implementation Steps

### 1. GitHub Integration
- Created a GitHub repository for application source code.
- Configured GitHub Webhook integration with Jenkins.
- Enabled automatic pipeline triggering when code is pushed.
- Used Git for version control.

### 2. Jenkins CI/CD Pipeline
- Installed and configured Jenkins on AWS EC2.
- Created a Jenkins pipeline using a Jenkinsfile.
- Configured source code checkout.
- Integrated SonarQube for code quality analysis.
- Automated Docker image building and pushing to AWS ECR.

### 3. SonarQube Integration
- Configured SonarQube on an Ubuntu EC2 instance.
- Integrated SonarQube with Jenkins.
- Performed automated code quality analysis.

### 4. Docker & AWS ECR
- Created a Docker image for the application.
- Configured an Amazon ECR repository.
- Authenticated Docker with AWS ECR.
- Tagged and pushed Docker images to ECR.

### 5. Amazon EKS & Kubernetes
- Created an Amazon EKS cluster.
- Configured a managed node group.
- Connected to the EKS cluster using kubectl.
- Created Kubernetes Deployment and Service manifests.
- Deployed the application with three replicas.
- Exposed the application through an AWS LoadBalancer.

### 6. Argo CD GitOps Integration
- Installed and configured Argo CD.
- Connected Argo CD to the GitHub repository containing Kubernetes manifests.
- Created an Argo CD application.
- Configured automated synchronization.
- Monitored deployment synchronization and application health.

## 🔄 CI/CD Workflow

1. Developer pushes application code to GitHub.
2. GitHub Webhook triggers Jenkins automatically.
3. Jenkins checks out the latest source code.
4. SonarQube performs code quality analysis.
5. Jenkins builds the Docker image.
6. Docker image is pushed to AWS ECR.
7. Updated Kubernetes manifests are committed to Git.
8. Argo CD detects the manifest changes and synchronizes the deployment to Amazon EKS.
9. Kubernetes manages the application Pods.
10. AWS LoadBalancer exposes the application externally.

